1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:08,908
The brush clears a carved elephant, large and steady. Elephants carried kings into battle, and carried honour into stone.

2
00:00:08,908 --> 00:00:16,121
The Chalukyas of Badami left many of them in their temples. What did an elephant stand for in a king's army?

3
00:00:33,183 --> 00:00:42,216
How do we know? The Aihole inscription, composed by the poet Ravikirti in 634 CE, praises Pulakeshin the Second.

4
00:00:42,216 --> 00:00:47,975
Archaeologists dug up and recorded the temples at Aihole, Badami and Pattadakal.

5
00:01:03,517 --> 00:01:13,310
The Chalukyas of Badami rose in Karnataka. Pulakeshin the First, about 543 CE, made Vatapi, now Badami, his capital.

6
00:01:13,310 --> 00:01:20,419
Pulakeshin the Second was the greatest king, ruling from about 610 to 642 CE.

7
00:01:20,419 --> 00:01:24,305
He defeated Harsha on the Narmada, and added much of the Deccan.

8
00:01:24,305 --> 00:01:30,712
The Aihole inscription of 634 CE, by the poet Ravikirti, praises his victories.

9
00:01:30,712 --> 00:01:40,246
In 642 CE, the Pallava king Narasimhavarman defeated him and entered Vatapi. Vikramaditya the First restored Chalukya power.

10
00:01:40,246 --> 00:01:48,723
The dynasty lasted until about 753 CE, when it fell to the Rashtrakutas. The Chalukyas were great builders.

11
00:01:48,723 --> 00:01:55,480
At Aihole, there are the Durga and Ladkhan temples. At Badami, there are rock-cut cave temples.

12
00:01:55,480 --> 00:02:02,719
At Pattadakal, the Virupaksha and Sangameshvara temples show a blend of northern and southern styles.

13
00:02:18,283 --> 00:02:27,644
Old name, new name. The Chalukya capital was called Vatapi in inscriptions. Today the town is called Badami, in Karnataka.

14
00:02:27,644 --> 00:02:33,324
It lies in a valley of red sandstone hills, and is famed for its cave temples.

15
00:02:48,883 --> 00:02:59,748
Carve this tip into memory. Vatapi, Pulakeshin II, Aihole. Pattadakal. A capital, a king, an inscription and a temple town.

16
00:02:59,748 --> 00:03:02,664
Four anchors for the Chalukyas.

17
00:03:18,217 --> 00:03:34,244
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Who composed the Aihole inscription? The answer: Ravikirti. Number two.

18
00:03:34,244 --> 00:03:51,430
What is Vatapi called today? The answer: Badami. Number three. At which site do the Virupaksha and Sangameshvara temples stand?

19
00:03:51,430 --> 00:04:01,286
The answer: Pattadakal. Now one line for Mains. Assess the role of the Chalukyas of Badami in Deccan history.

20
00:04:20,850 --> 00:04:28,789
The elephant goes into its case. It stands for the power of the early Deccan kings. Three facts to take away.

21
00:04:28,789 --> 00:04:42,334
A carved elephant of the Deccan. Chalukyas of Badami, 543 to 753 CE. Builders at Aihole and Pattadakal. Next gallery: The Cholas.

